{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "the social network is a film that masterfully dissects the complexities of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success. directed by david fincher, the movie tells the story of mark zuckerberg, the creator of facebook, and the tumultuous journey that led him to become one of the most influential people in the world.\n\nthe film begins with mark, a sophomore at harvard, being dumped by his girlfriend, erica albright. this rejection sparks a chain of events that ultimately leads to the creation of facebook. mark, along with his college roommates eduardo saverin, andrew mc collum, dustin moskovitz, and chris hughes, launch thefacebook, a social networking site exclusively for harvard students.\n\nas the site gains popularity, mark's life becomes increasingly complicated. he is sued by twin brothers cameron and tyler winklevoss, who claim that mark stole their idea for a social networking site. the film's portrayal of the lawsuit is gripping, with the winklevoss twins coming across as entitled and arrogant, while mark is shown to be cunning and ruthless in his pursuit of success.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its exploration of the complex relationships between the characters. mark's friendship with eduardo is particularly noteworthy, as it highlights the tension between loyalty and ambition. eduardo, who initially helps mark fund the site, becomes increasingly disenchanted with mark's growing success and the two eventually become estranged.\n\nthe film's soundtrack, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, perfectly captures the mood of the movie. the score is haunting and atmospheric, with a blend of electronic and orchestral elements that adds to the overall tension and unease.\n\nthe cast delivers outstanding performances, with jesse eisenberg shining as mark zuckerberg. eisenberg's portrayal of mark is nuanced 